WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.558
A persistence model describes what the
database can store.

2
00:00:03.638 --> 00:00:06.451
A domain model describes business state
and behavior.

3
00:00:06.531 --> 00:00:10.323
A request contract describes exactly what
one public operation accepts.

4
00:00:10.403 --> 00:00:14.684
Reusing one representation for all three
couples external capability to internal

5
00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:15.376
structure.

6
00:00:15.456 --> 00:00:16.863
When the database evolves,

7
00:00:16.943 --> 00:00:21.162
an API can acquire new writable fields
without an intentional security decision.

8
00:00:21.242 --> 00:00:25.707
A profile-edit contract might contain only
optional displayName and timezone fields

9
00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:29.090
even though the user table also stores
role, tenant membership,

10
00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.839
verification state, lock status, billing
data, and audit timestamps.

11
00:00:32.919 --> 00:00:37.078
That narrow type communicates the
operation's design to reviewers and callers.

12
00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:40.766
Separate types for password change,
administrative account update,

13
00:00:40.846 --> 00:00:45.189
project settings, and ownership transfer
make their different authority visible.

14
00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:48.021
Partial of User is usually the wrong
public contract.

15
00:00:48.101 --> 00:00:52.076
Partial preserves every property in User
and merely makes each one optional.

16
00:00:52.156 --> 00:00:56.254
It answers whether a property must be
present, not whether a caller may write it.

17
00:00:56.334 --> 00:00:58.964
Generated ORM update types have the same
mismatch:

18
00:00:59.044 --> 00:01:03.019
they describe persistence operations the
library can technically express,

19
00:01:03.099 --> 00:01:05.301
including relations or nested mutations,

20
00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:08.928
rather than the fields one API operation
should delegate to a client.

21
00:01:09.008 --> 00:01:13.106
Compile-time excess-property checks on
some object literals are not a runtime

22
00:01:13.186 --> 00:01:15.938
boundary and do not filter JSON parsed
from a request.

23
00:01:16.018 --> 00:01:19.626
Define contracts from the use case
outward, not from the table inward.

24
00:01:19.706 --> 00:01:23.682
A request DTO can share small value types
with the domain where that is useful,

25
00:01:23.762 --> 00:01:26.208
but its property set should remain
deliberate.

26
00:01:26.288 --> 00:01:30.447
Keep response models separate as well so
writable input is not inferred from what

27
00:01:30.527 --> 00:01:31.383
the API returns.

28
00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:34.643
This separation provides a reviewable
capability boundary:

29
00:01:34.723 --> 00:01:38.882
developers can see which state the
endpoint accepts before tracing validation,

30
00:01:38.962 --> 00:01:42.020
authorization, business rules, and
persistence mapping.
